.TH CREATE_CTHREAD_POOL_MP 3 "2026-08-02" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
create_cthread_pool_mp \- create a thread pool with custom memory management
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cthreadpool.h>

ctpool create_cthread_pool_mp(size_t num_threads,
                              size_t queue_capacity,
                              ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mprocs,
                              char **err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a new thread pool with the specified number of worker threads.
The queue_capacity parameter controls the task queue mode:

  0 or ccol_invalid_size -> unbounded task queue. ctpool_submit never
    blocks on capacity; the only non-argument failure path is
    ccol_not_enough_memory.

  N > 0 -> bounded task queue of capacity N. ctpool_submit blocks when
    the queue is full; ctpool_try_submit returns ccol_container_full
    instead of blocking.

All num_threads worker threads are started before the function returns.
If any pthread_create call fails the already-started threads are joined
cleanly before the function returns CTPOOL_INVALID.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
num_threads    Number of worker threads (must be >= 1)
queue_capacity 0 or ccol_invalid_size for unbounded; N > 0 for bounded
mprocs         Custom allocator, or NULL for malloc/free
err_str        Optional: receives a static error string on failure
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a new ctpool handle on success.
Returns CTPOOL_INVALID on failure and sets *err_str if err_str is
non-NULL.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The returned handle is an opaque VALUE (a packed {slot index,
generation} pair), not a pointer; never cast it to/from void* or
treat it as an address.
Futures always use standard malloc/free regardless of mprocs.
The small internal task node backing each submission is recycled
through a bounded per-pool cache rather than allocated and freed via
mprocs on every single call; a caller relying on mprocs to observe
one allocation and one free per submission (for example a tracking or
instrumenting allocator) will instead see calls only when the cache is
empty or already full, not on every call.
The pool must be shut down with ctpool_shutdown_drain or
ctpool_shutdown_immediate before calling ctpool_destroy, unless
ctpool_destroy is used directly (which performs a drain shutdown).
__ctpool_destroy waits for every in-flight resolved use of the handle
(a concurrently executing ctpool_submit/_wait/_pending_count/... call)
to finish before freeing anything, so the caller does not need to
independently prove no other thread is still using the pool; see
__ctpool_destroy(3).

A fresh create_cthread_pool_mp/ctpool_submit/_try_submit/_timed_submit/
ctpool_destroy (and the future/wait/shutdown variants) call, from any
thread, in either the parent or a freshly forked child, never hangs on
a lock some other (possibly no-longer-existing, since fork(2)
duplicates only the calling thread) thread happened to hold at the
instant of the fork. A pool already live across the fork is a
different matter: its worker threads are not duplicated into the
child, so an inherited pool can no longer run any queued or future
task there regardless of this guarantee; treat such a pool as inert in
the child (not usable for further submission). ctpool_wait on such a
pool in the child returns immediately rather than waiting for progress
that can never happen, and ctpool_destroy still discards any work
still queued at the moment of the fork (cancelling any queued futures,
so a caller blocked in ctpool_future_get on one of them wakes up
rather than hanging) before releasing the pool's own memory, even with
no prior explicit shutdown call.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR create_cthread_pool (3),
.BR ctpool_construct (3),
.BR __ctpool_destroy (3),
.BR ctpool_shutdown_drain (3),
.BR ctpool_shutdown_immediate (3)
